WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.190 align:start position:0%
 
now when the first  covert 19 wave hit india millions of  migrant workers lost their jobs  strict lockdowns meant their source of  income in the cities was cut off  overnight  forcing many to move back to their  villages nearly a year  on many are still there but for rural  communities  it's been a blessing in disguise with  the returning migrant workers breathing  new life into their economies  the pandemic cost him his job  kanaram garasiya was working far from  home when the lockdown came  it took him four months to get back to  his village in rajasthan state  there he had family but no paid work  then he heard of training courses from  migrant laborers in the nearby city of  odaipur  now he's learning to operate a sewing  machine  before the lockdown i worked as a stone  mason with granite in the city of surat  i was there from 2012. the lockdown  announcement came so  suddenly everything was shut down and  prime minister modi said the government  would take care of us  but nothing happened we weren't even  allowed out on the street  the police would beat us with their  batons as soon as we left the house  for a thousand rupees or 11 euros the  step academy provides a one month  training course  including accommodation and meals  as well as textile work the school  offers a wide range of courses  from hairdressing to electrical  installation  as well as repairs to mobile phones and  cars  the school director believes the return  of migrant workers is good for the local  economy  because of the pandemic we've trained  around a thousand young people  many of them are found work locally or  open businesses  it's creating jobs because employers and  business owners are returning to the  villages  the step academy also partners with  local enterprises  who provide on-the-job training like  here on this construction site  the trainees earn a minimum wage and can  be fully qualified in 12 months  i worked in a kitchen in gujarat and  earned 5 000 rupees or  55 euros a month i had to work non-stop  even nights i came home just before the  lockdown and planned to stay here  the work is better and so are the wages  i'm home in the mornings and evenings  so i can help out with the housework  tejo ram is happy to be back on the land  despite the hardships  he and his wife live in a remote village  which they can only reach on foot  cities are okay but they're loud and  dirty  i'd much rather live in the village it's  better here  month of training is coming to an end  he's now contacting local tailors who  might offer him a job  he's determined to stay close to his  family and not return to surat or any  other large city to seek work  if i went far away again i couldn't look  after my children so i'd rather look for  work close to my village  and after a visit to a local shop his  hopes are high  the owner wanted proof of his sewing  skills and thanks to his training  kannaram was able to show him  once his course is over he's been  promised there'll be a job waiting for  him  well for more now i'm joined by dw  correspondent nemisha jaiswal  in delhi namisha not all migrant workers  displaced by the pandemic have  access to vocational training tell us  more about the scale  of this displacement and how the  pandemic has affected  migrant workers in general in india  well terry the impact on migrant workers  across the country  has been immense from delhi alone the  government estimates  that some 800 000 migrant workers left  during the second wave  and the ensuing curfews but many of  these people  told us that even back home they  struggled to find work  food was hard to come across and health  care of course  is not as good as it is in the cities  even for those who stayed back because  the curfew was extended week upon week  in many cities they struggled with  hunger and there was a problem with the  government rolling out provisions  efficiently as well  now curfews are gradually being lifted  but even going forward given the  prediction of a third wave  there is a high sense of insecurity  because many are still paying off the  debts they incurred  during the first wave so what if where  do things stand now  with the pandemic in india are  infections still rising are hospitals  able to cope  well infections are definitely coming  down terry they have  dipped below 50 000 that is still a very  high number  but given the comparison to the piece  that had crossed 400  000 things are improving the death toll  also is gradually declining however  some states are revising their death  toll numbers saying that they could have  been under counting  during the second wave and once again  with curfews listing governments are  emphatically saying  that please do not lack become lacks  about precautions  already the reports of markets seeing  surges in customers  so there are considerable regional  differences i understand what about  vaccinations how is the vaccine rollout  coming along  across india  well there is some positive news when it  comes to the vaccine rollout terry it  opened up for everybody above 18 in may  but the uptake was low and the supplies  were very low  finally supplies are improving the  government has recently revised its  policies for the state  once again and yesterday india actually  crossed  8 million doses in single day if this  trend continues  there is still hope that the country is  able to fight off the third wave or at  least diminish its impact  nimisha thank you very much for your  update as always that was dw's namisha  jaiswal in delhi  you

